1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:09,940
Welcome to, uh, The Deep Dive. Today, our mission is exploring how you, the government official running your ministry systems, can actually keep your national enterprise

2
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:11,680
architecture repository true.

3
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:14,940
Right. The EA, or P-A-E-R-A as it's often called.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,720
Yeah, exactly. And, you know, we really need to establish that this repository has one ultimate enemy.

5
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:23,260
It's not a technical glitch or a cyberattack.

6
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:25,780
It's s, um... It's staleness.

7
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:28,640
Oh, absolutely. Staleness is the silent killer here.

8
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:33,730
Because an architecture that is, say, six months behind reality is actually worse than none at all.

9
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:36,560
People trust it, and it basically lies to them.

10
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:38,220
Yeah, that's exactly the danger.

11
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:44,320
So why is an architecture that's six months out of date actually worse than having no architecture at all?

12
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:46,449
I mean, shouldn't some map be better than no map?

13
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:50,600
Well, it comes down to how we behave. A missing architecture makes people ask questions.

14
00:00:50,910 --> 00:00:51,680
If there's no map,

15
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,960
they, uh, they go find out how things actually connect.

16
00:00:55,140 --> 00:00:56,130
Right. They do a legwork.

17
00:00:56,440 --> 00:01:01,180
Exactly. But a wrong architecture that answers their questions, it just answers them wrongly.

18
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:03,380
Oh, wow, so they feel confident when they shouldn't.

19
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:04,339
Precisely.

20
00:01:05,019 --> 00:01:09,960
Just imagine your repository claims the examination authority consumes the learner register.

21
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:11,680
Okay. A pretty standard setup.

22
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:15,240
Yeah. But in reality, they quietly built their own,

23
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:21,590
um, duplicated, siloed copy of that register last quarter.

24
00:01:21,790 --> 00:01:24,170
Wait, really? Just went rogue and built their own?

25
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:31,080
It happens all the time. And if your repository doesn't show that, then every ministry decision is being built on a falsehood.

26
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:33,080
Currency isn't just a nice-to-have feature.

27
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:34,760
It is the whole value.

28
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:37,900
Because you'd be making decisions based on a ghost system.

29
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:41,220
Right. Okay, so if currency is the entire value,

30
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:47,220
how does a ministry actually operationalize this without it becoming a massive administrative chore?

31
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:49,880
Like, who should exactly be responsible for updating it?

32
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:52,770
Well, there must be one named owner. Usually, that's your chief architect.

33
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:54,860
Okay, just one person, not a committee.

34
00:01:55,260 --> 00:01:58,380
Definitely not a committee. Okay. Because, you know, when everyone owns currency, no one does.

35
00:01:58,780 --> 00:02:01,770
That makes total sense. But how do we schedule these updates?

36
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:04,140
I mean, please don't tell me it's some sort of annual audit.

37
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:10,400
No, no, absolutely not. A yearly calendar review basically guarantees the EA is 11 months stale.

38
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:14,960
Yeah, useless. Instead, you have to tie updates to real-world events.

39
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:18,780
There are four specific event-driven triggers you have to use.

40
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:19,700
Okay, what's number one?

41
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:20,240
Number one,

42
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:22,140
a system goes live.

43
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,940
When that happens, you immediately update the application layer.

44
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:27,380
Makes sense. Number two,

45
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:29,660
a system is retired,

46
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:32,220
so you just remove it.

47
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:33,860
Out with the old. Got it.

48
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:34,840
Right. Number three,

49
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,280
a new data domain appears.

50
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:40,460
You add it, and crucially, you add it with its owner.

51
00:02:40,780 --> 00:02:43,250
Okay. What's the last one? Number four. Yep.

52
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:47,460
The board makes a decision. You record that decision along with its reason.

53
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:49,140
Let me push back on this a little bit, though.

54
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:54,300
If updates are tied to all these different events, doesn't that risk filling the repository with private language?

55
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:54,960
Oh, for sure.

56
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,700
Like, you just get orphaned items and fragmented data as different teams log their own changes, right?

57
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:06,000
And that is the exact risk, and the solution to that is a light gate, a checkpoint you implement during board reviews.

58
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:07,460
A gate at the board level.

59
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:10,920
Yeah. It's the absolute cheapest moment to update.

60
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:16,140
Oh. Because a project is already detailing what it will build, what it will consume, and what it will touch.

61
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:19,380
Oh, right. Their bags are already open for inspection, so to speak.

62
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:23,340
Exactly. So at this gate, you do three quick checks.

63
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:27,020
Number one, does the update use the shared entities correctly?

64
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:32,140
Basically making sure they're using the right building blocks and not inventing new terms.

65
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:37,580
Right. Keep the language standard. Number two, does every data domain still have exactly one owner?

66
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:41,600
Okay. And number three, is every decision logged with its reason?

67
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:48,380
That is so clean. It makes the governance process and the update discipline just a single unified motion.

68
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:52,220
It does. It prevents the whole thing from becoming costly to change later on.

69
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:55,220
It's brilliant because it keeps the repository true.

70
00:03:55,280 --> 00:04:00,880
You have one named owner, event-triggered updates, and this light conformance check at the board level.

71
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:01,980
That's the discipline.

72
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:11,390
And if you neglect that discipline, within a year, you don't just have a flawed IT tool, you have a widely trusted work of fiction driving your ministry's most costly

73
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:12,040
decisions.

74
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:13,560
Which is terrifying, honestly.

